---
import Evidence from './Evidence.astro'

/* Who I am and what I do, and nothing else.
 *
 * This used to be wrapped in the seam — one block of display type rendered
 * twice, the second copy clipped to the far side of a percentage so the
 * headline was cut mid-word. It was the best-looking thing on the site and it
 * is gone, deliberately. A seam is a *boundary*, and the argument this site
 * makes is that there is no boundary: the front end and the server have been
 * two ends of one job since the job existed. The vertical line is still here,
 * but it now runs the length of every page instead of one screen, and it says
 * the opposite thing — see src/components/Spine.astro.
 *
 * The prose is unchanged from the version before it, on purpose. The home page
 * got shorter by moving the career, the practice inventory and the
 * endorsements to /about, not by rewriting sentences that were already doing
 * their job.
 */

const beats = [
	{ k: 'Front-End', v: 'Last eleven years' },
	{ k: 'React', v: 'Eight of those' },
	{ k: 'Back-End', v: 'Eight years' },
	{ k: 'Python', v: 'Returning' },
	{ k: 'AI', v: 'Building with it' }
]
---

<section class="shell shell-river pb-section-tight pt-32 sm:pt-40" id="home">
	<p class="label tick mb-7" data-enter style="--enter:0">Ariel Gianatiempo · Málaga, Spain</p>

	<h1 class="max-w-title text-display leading-[0.93] tracking-[-0.03em]" data-enter style="--enter:1">
		Nineteen years making complicated software easier to <em class="italic text-accent">use</em>.
	</h1>

	<!-- Layer two around layer three: the account at measure, the receipts
	     raised into it. The prose runs down the panel's flank and closes
	     underneath it across the full width of the section rather than stopping
	     short of it. See `.runaround` in tokens.css.

	     23rem where /about uses 31rem: this panel's longest row needs 285px and
	     about's Languages row needs 401px. Only the panel is sized here — the
	     text takes whatever is left beside it, so the two pages differ by the
	     one measurement that actually differs.

	     The panel leads in source order because a float only affects what
	     follows it; the `--enter` index still lands it last in the entrance. -->
	<div class="runaround mt-11" style="--ra-panel:23rem">
		<Evidence class="runaround-panel" label="At a glance" bodyClass="px-6 sm:px-7" data-enter style="--enter:4">
			<dl>
				{
					beats.map((beat) => (
						<div class="flex min-w-0 flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule py-4 first:border-t-0">
							<dt class="label text-accent">{beat.k}</dt>
							<dd class="label label-value text-primary">{beat.v}</dd>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</dl>
		</Evidence>

		<div class="space-y-5 text-body-lg text-secondary">
			<p data-enter style="--enter:2">
				Eight years of web development with Java, then eleven on the Front-End. Openbank's homebanking, J.P. Morgan tooling, security products at V3 Cybersecurity. Different industries, but the job
				underneath was always the same: take something dense and confusing, and get a person to the point where they can actually do something with it.
			</p>
			<p data-enter style="--enter:3">
				I'm choosing to start over, Python rather than Java this time, and going further into <strong class="font-medium">building products with AI inside them</strong>. That's not prompting. There is
				plenty of prompting going on while I build my first projects with Python, AI and a whole new set of problems to solve for companies and users alike, but the two are not the same job.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
